// levels.bs -- a string from outside becomes a member of a named set, or
// nothing (F39, ticket 10 §4).
//
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Levels Parse "\"warn\""
//         -> :warn
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Levels Parse "\"nope\""
//         -> :nothing
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Levels OrElse "\"nope\"" :info
//         -> :info
//
// THE PROPERTY WORTH LOOKING AT is that this never touches the atom table.
// `ParseAtom<T>` is a CODEGEN OBLIGATION, like `ValidateAs<T>` next door in
// `examples/Intake`: the compiler reads the type argument, enumerates T's
// members, and emits one match arm per member returning that member as a
// LITERAL --
//
//     case S of
//         <<"debug">> -> debug;
//         ...
//         _           -> nothing
//     end
//
// -- so no `binary_to_existing_atom` is called and a string built at run time
// cannot grow the atom table through this path. Erlang's own safe spelling has
// to ask the table, because it does not know the permitted set; this does,
// because the type argument IS the permitted set.
//
// The second effect is the one ticket 10 did not anticipate. Because the
// members are emitted in VALUE position, they are interned by construction --
// a union whose members appear only in a type has no such guarantee.
//
// AND THE RESIDUAL IS ORDINARY DATA. A string naming no member is `:nothing`,
// which is why the return is `option<Level>` and not `Level`: the compiler
// refuses the narrower signature and offers the wider one. `OrElse` below is
// what reading it looks like -- a switch, not a rescue, because nothing was
// thrown.
module Levels

// A finite atom union. `ParseAtom<T>` requires exactly this: a cofinite `T`
// such as `atom` has no member list to enumerate and is an error at the call,
// and so is a union carrying anything that is not an atom -- `:debug | int`
// would promise an `int` the parse could never return.
type Level = :debug | :info | :warn | :error

// `option<T>` is the prelude's name for `T | :nothing` (ticket 10 §5). It
// creates no type -- `option<Level>` and `Level | :nothing` are the same set --
// so this signature is the honest one written the readable way.
public option<Level> Parse(string s)

Parse(s) -> ParseAtom<Level>(s)

// Handling the residual is branching, because the failure is a value. There is
// no crash to catch and no error payload to inspect: the parse either names a
// member or does not, and a caller that has a default says so here.
public Level OrElse(string s, Level fallback)

OrElse(s, fallback) -> Parse(s) switch {
    :nothing => fallback,
    level    => level
}
